<article class="env-exercise" id="02A2">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02A2">Exercise <span data-tag="02A2">111.37.6</span>.</a> Maps of $\text{Proj}$. Let $R$ and $S$ be graded rings. Suppose we have a ring map </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \psi : R \to S  \]
</div>
  <p> and an integer $e \geq 1$ such that $\psi (R_ d) \subset S_{de}$ for all $d \geq 0$. (By our conventions this is not a homomorphism of graded rings, unless $e = 1$.) </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
For which elements $\mathfrak p \in \text{Proj}(S)$ is there a well-defined corresponding point in $\text{Proj}(R)$? In other words, find a suitable open $U \subset \text{Proj}(S)$ such that $\psi $ defines a continuous map $r_\psi : U \to \text{Proj}(R)$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Give an example where $U \not= \text{Proj}(S)$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Give an example where $U = \text{Proj}(S)$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
(Do not write this down.) Convince yourself that the continuous map $U \to \text{Proj}(R)$ comes canonically with a map on sheaves so that $r_\psi $ is a morphism of schemes: 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \text{Proj}(S) \supset U \longrightarrow \text{Proj}(R).  \]
</div>
</li><li><p>
What can you say about this map if $R = \bigoplus _{d \geq 0} S_{de}$ (as a graded ring with $S_ e$, $S_{2e}$, etc in degree $1$, $2$, etc) and $\psi $ is the inclusion mapping? 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>